{"model":"kimi-k3:cloud","prompt":"Author exactly one round's reference patch and behavioral checks for the fixed\nfresh coding project below. You are kimi-k3:cloud. Output one complete JSON object\nonly, without Markdown fences, commentary, schema placeholders or partial data.\n\nProject ID (JSON): \"staged-03\"\nCurrent round (integer): 1\nImmutable scaffold with assembled original messages/requests (JSON):\n{\"description\":\"Normalize raw document metadata dicts into canonical records: whitespace-cleaned strings, deduplicated authors and tags, a derived slug, author counts and a visibility label. Three rounds evolve normalize_record (new derived fields and a visibility branch) and derive_slug (retiring edition suffixes in favor of version suffixes, then kind namespacing with a length cap), with derive_slug revisited.\",\"initial_file\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"text\":\"\\\"\\\"\\\"Normalize document metadata records into a canonical form.\\n\\nA record is a plain dict describing one document. Known keys include\\ntitle, publisher, series, authors, tags, edition and draft. Every\\nhelper is pure: nothing mutates the incoming record, and results are\\nfresh dicts or strings.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef clean_strings(record):\\n    fixed = dict(record)\\n    for key in (\\\"title\\\", \\\"publisher\\\", \\\"series\\\"):\\n        value = fixed.get(key)\\n        if isinstance(value, str):\\n            fixed[key] = \\\" \\\".join(value.split())\\n    return fixed\\n\\n\\ndef normalize_authors(record):\\n    fixed = dict(record)\\n    authors = fixed.get(\\\"authors\\\")\\n    if authors is None:\\n        return fixed\\n    if isinstance(authors, str):\\n        authors = [authors]\\n    cleaned = []\\n    for name in authors:\\n        if not isinstance(name, str):\\n            continue\\n        name = \\\" \\\".join(name.split())\\n        if name and name not in cleaned:\\n            cleaned.append(name)\\n    fixed[\\\"authors\\\"] = cleaned\\n    return fixed\\n\\n\\ndef normalize_tags(record):\\n    fixed = dict(record)\\n    tags = fixed.get(\\\"tags\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(tags, list):\\n        return fixed\\n    cleaned = []\\n    for tag in tags:\\n        if not isinstance(tag, str):\\n            continue\\n        tag = tag.lower().replace(\\\"_\\\", \\\" \\\").strip()\\n        tag = \\\"-\\\".join(tag.split())\\n        if tag and tag not in cleaned:\\n            cleaned.append(tag)\\n    fixed[\\\"tags\\\"] = cleaned\\n    return fixed\\n\\n\\ndef derive_slug(record):\\n    title = record.get(\\\"title\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(title, str) or not title.strip():\\n        title = \\\"untitled\\\"\\n    words = []\\n    for word in title.lower().split():\\n        word = \\\"\\\".join([ch for ch in word if ch.isalnum()])\\n        if word:\\n            words.append(word)\\n    if not words:\\n        words = [\\\"untitled\\\"]\\n    slug = \\\"-\\\".join(words)\\n    edition = record.get(\\\"edition\\\")\\n    if isinstance(edition, int) and edition > 1:\\n        slug = slug + \\\"-ed\\\" + str(edition)\\n    return slug\\n\\n\\ndef normalize_record(record):\\n    stage = clean_strings(record)\\n    stage = normalize_authors(stage)\\n    stage = normalize_tags(stage)\\n    result = dict(stage)\\n    result[\\\"slug\\\"] = derive_slug(stage)\\n    result[\\\"author_count\\\"] = len(result.get(\\\"authors\\\", []))\\n    if result.get(\\\"draft\\\") is True:\\n        result[\\\"visibility\\\"] = \\\"internal\\\"\\n    else:\\n        result[\\\"visibility\\\"] = \\\"public\\\"\\n    return result\\n\"},\"project_id\":\"staged-03\",\"rounds\":[{\"index\":1,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m05\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update normalize_record in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m01\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"We're wiring normalize_record into our ingestion job for document metadata. Keep the current pipeline shape: clean_strings, normalize_authors, normalize_tags, then slug, author_count and visibility, all on a fresh dict. Add a title_words field holding the number of whitespace-separated words in the cleaned title, or 0 when the title is missing or not a string. {'title': '  A  B '} must end with title_words 2.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m02\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Product wants primary_author: the first entry of the normalized authors list, or None when that list is empty. author_count stays the length of the same list, so primary_author is None exactly when author_count is 0. Also make sure the authors key is always present in the output as a list, even when the input had none. Someone cited a wiki line, 'normalize_record lowercases every author name' — never implemented, don't start; author case stays untouched.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m03\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"One new visibility branch: after tag normalization, when 'embargo' is among the tags, visibility becomes 'restricted'. Draft records still win with 'internal' even if embargoed, and everything else remains 'public'. This adds the embargo condition only; it does not alter the draft rule, the public default, or how tags are cleaned.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m04\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Boundary that keeps biting us: normalize_record({}) must yield slug 'untitled', authors [], author_count 0, title_words 0, primary_author None, visibility 'public', and leave the input dict untouched. The slug must still come from derive_slug called on the cleaned record, and the helper pipeline order stays as it is.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\"}},{\"index\":2,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m10\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update derive_slug in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m06\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"derive_slug needs rework. Retire the edition handling completely: edition is ignored from now on, so {'title': 'Cooking', 'edition': 3} yields 'cooking', never 'cooking-ed3'. It is replaced by a version key: when version is a non-empty string, append '-' plus its lowercased alphanumerics, so {'title': 'Cooking', 'version': 'Draft 2'} gives 'cooking-draft2'.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m07\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Details on the version token: lowercase the string, keep only letters and digits, so 'v1.2 RC' contributes '-v12rc'. If nothing survives (version '...'), or version is not a string, skip the suffix. The existing fallbacks are unchanged: a blank or missing title, or a title whose words all strip to nothing, still bases the slug on 'untitled'.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m08\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Cap the title part at 8 words before any version suffix is attached, so long titles yield at most 8 hyphenated words and the suffix is never cut off. Our style sheet says 'slugs should read naturally' — deliberately vague, ignore it; the rules in this thread are the spec.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m09\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"normalize_record calls derive_slug, so all of this flows through automatically; do not special-case that caller. {'title': '', 'version': '2'} must give 'untitled-2'. Old records will keep carrying edition data for a while; it simply must not influence slugs in any way.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"derive_slug\"}},{\"index\":3,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m15\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update derive_slug in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m11\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"derive_slug once more: we now namespace by document kind. When kind is a non-empty string, normalize it like a slug token (lowercase, letters and digits only) and prepend it with '--' ahead of the title part: {'kind': 'Blog Post', 'title': 'Hi'} becomes 'blogpost--hi'. A kind that cleans to nothing is skipped. This is additive; version suffixing, the 8-word cap and the untitled fallbacks do not change.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m12\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Cap the finished slug at 60 characters total. Clip kind and version tokens to 20 characters each when normalizing; if the assembled slug is still too long, drop title words from the end until it fits, never cutting a word in half and never sacrificing the kind prefix or the version suffix. If even the first title word cannot fit, the middle falls back to 'untitled'.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m13\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Edition stays retired. A teammate floated bringing back the '-ed2' suffixes 'for nostalgia' in chat; that is not happening, edition still has zero effect on slugs, including when kind prefixes are present. The untitled fallbacks and the 8-word cap from the last change also remain in force.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m14\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Lock in these outcomes: {'kind': 'note', 'title': 'a b', 'version': 'V2'} gives 'note--a-b-v2'; {'kind': '!!!', 'title': 'Report'} gives 'report'; {'kind': 'zine', 'edition': 5, 'title': 'Ink'} gives 'zine--ink'. Kind handling lives strictly inside derive_slug; nothing about authors, tags, title_words or visibility changes because of it.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"derive_slug\"}}]}\nImmutable earlier accepted round packets for this same project (JSON):\n[]\n\nLineage: these are author-created materials for this same fresh project, not\ncoding-worker answers. Do not import any other project or benchmark example.\nDo not edit or restate the scaffold or prior packets in your output. No validator\nfeedback or correction attempt is provided or permitted.\n\nFor rounds1 and2 output exactly:\n{\"reference_patch\": string, \"public_checks\": [check objects],\n \"private_checks\": [check objects]}.\nFor round3 output those same keys and additionally\n\"obsolete_mutant\": {\"source\": string, \"failing_check_id\": string}.\nUse the exact check object fields and semantics in the appended data contract.\nThe assembler supplies round indices and separates public/private fields.\n\nThe complete future schedule is visible ONLY so you can set check expiry\nintervals correctly. Current expected behavior and the reference must follow\nonly instructions visible through this round: m01..m05 in round1, m01..m10 in\nround2, m01..m15 in round3. Never anticipate a future change. Citing an earlier\nmessage does not justify an expectation that actually depends on a future one.\nUse explicit source-grounded rationales, including cancellation scope and\nindependent conditions that survive. All allowed outputs must remain accepted.\n\nProduce only a replacement for this round's existing target function. Earlier\nreference patches apply sequentially to the initial module; do not reset or\nchange other functions. Keep the replacement comfortably within1024 output\ntokens including JSON escaping and128tokens of headroom. Do not add imports,\nannotations, decorators, nested functions or top-level execution.\n\nAdd compact, meaningful public and private cases without duplicating old cases\nthat remain active. Each round introduces at least two private cases including\nnew functionality. At each round, at least two active private cases must cover\nretained behavior; rounds2 and3 also need at least two active private retirement\ncases. Coverage may reuse earlier active cases exactly as the contract permits.\nInclude conditional interactions and boundaries. Check IDs are unique across\nthis project. Introduction is the current round; expiry is inclusive or null.\nAvoid overlapping conflicting expected results. A changed expectation gets a\nnew ID; the earlier case's frozen expiry must already make this consistent.\n\nAt round3, the additional mutant must be a valid replacement for the final\ntarget that incorrectly preserves an actually retired rule. It must fail its\nnamed active PRIVATE retirement check, while the correct final reference passes.\nDo not provide a mutant in earlier rounds.\n\nReason through the accumulated reference state and all active checks before\nresponding. Do not claim you executed Python unless you actually did. Local\nexecution will validate your artifacts afterward; a failure stops preparation.\n\nThe following unchanged check/reference contract is authoritative for the\nassembled project. Return only this round packet's fields above, not a whole\nproject object:\n# Source replay project data, schema 1 (prospective)\n\nOne JSON object per independently Kimi-authored project. No prose outside JSON.\nNo old examples or model responses are authoring inputs. Exact keys below;\nreject extra keys, duplicate IDs, nonfinite numbers and invalid types.\n\nTop level: schema_version (1), author (\"kimi-k3:cloud\"), lineage (nonempty\ndescription of fresh independent authorship; no fit or benchmark reuse), public,\nprivate. Public/private are separate projections in the consumer; runtime\nbuilders take public only. Never include private objects in serialized prompts.\n\npublic:\n- project_id: fixed by the independent authoring request, e.g. replay-01.\n- description: brief project purpose.\n- initial_file: {path: \"module.py\", text: complete Python module}.\n- rounds: exactly three objects {index, source_messages, request, target,\n  public_checks}. index is 1,2,3 in order.\n\nsource_messages: exactly four {message_id, role, text} per round. role is \"user\".\nrequest: {message_id, role: \"user\", text}. IDs are unique m01 through m15 in\nconversation order; four source messages then one request per round. Each text\nis nonempty and at most 640 UTF-8 bytes. Array order defines chronology.\ntarget: {path: \"module.py\", symbol: existing top-level function name}.\nAt least one target is revisited. Do not include assistant/tool transcripts.\n\nInitial module contains only ordinary synchronous pure Python functions, with\none positional argument each; optional module docstring is allowed. No imports,\nfile/network access, decorators, nesting, annotations or top-level computation.\nThe existing action/sandbox restrictions remain authoritative. At most 65536\nUTF-8 bytes for initial module, each replacement function and each evolving\nmodule. References should comfortably fit the fixed 1024-token worker allowance;\npreflight measures native encoding with at least 128 output tokens of headroom.\nHelpers may call one another; target names and non-target code must survive edits.\n\npublic_checks: list of check objects first introduced at this round. Checks do\nnot require future functions to be implemented. Public checks are visible to\nworkers when introduced; all active public checks accompany later work.\nThe runtime projection contains only check_id, symbol, input, expected_values.\nAll other check fields are controller/audit metadata, never model-visible or\nincluded in public tool feedback. In particular, future interval endpoints and\nauthor-written source rationales must not leak through the public projection.\nThe reused sandbox receives those four executable fields plus internal rule_ids=[];\nthis is a compatibility adapter, not an authored obligation list.\n\nprivate: {rounds, obsolete_mutant}.\nprivate.rounds: exactly three {index, reference_patch, checks} objects.\nreference_patch is one complete replacement function for the corresponding\npublic target. Sequential reference application must pass all active public and\nprivate checks at every round via the actual worker consumer/sandbox.\nchecks are hidden cases first introduced at that round.\nobsolete_mutant: {source, failing_check_id}; source replaces the final target in\nthe reference final workspace, compiles and is accepted by the action consumer,\nbut incorrectly preserves a retired rule. It must fail the named private\nretirement check while the correct final reference passes it. This is a\ncounterexample to test vacuity, not a fitted worker or a gold source selection.\n\nEvery public/private check has exact fields:\n{check_id, symbol, input, expected_values, active_from_round,\n active_through_round, source_ids, behavior, rationale}.\ncheck_id is globally unique within a project. symbol is an existing function.\ninput and every expected_values element are finite JSON values. expected_values\nis a nonempty list of all accepted outputs for that case, compared using the\nexisting strict JSON equality. No implementation-string/style assertions.\nactive_from_round equals its introduction index; active_through_round is null\n(through round 3) or an integer from active_from_round through 3, inclusive.\nsource_ids are nonempty unique source/request IDs visible at introduction.\nbehavior is \"functionality\", \"retained\", or \"retirement\". rationale explains\nwhy these sources require or permit the accepted behavior for this input.\nAn initial functionality check remaining active becomes retained coverage on\nlater rounds; coverage is derived from introduction and current round as well\nas the tag. Tags alone do not prove a retained rule or a retirement was tested.\n\nEach round introduces at least two private cases, with at least one new\nfunctionality case. Each round has at least two active private cases for retained\nbehavior (round 1 may derive these from the starting module's specified behavior\nand initial source instructions). Rounds 2 and 3 have at least two active private\nretirement cases. Across each project's three rounds, include genuine unseen\ninputs, conditional interactions and boundary cases. A case changing expected\nbehavior has a new check ID and interval. Conflicting active expected outputs\nfor the same (symbol,input) are invalid. Independent source review must check\nwhether a check is justified, whether a permitted choice is incorrectly forced,\nand whether an allegedly retired condition actually ceased to apply.\n\nNo manual recap, oracle obligation list, effective-rule label, gold selected ID,\nmodel answer or benchmark field exists. The preflight receipt binds exact public,\nprivate, reference execution, mutant failure and source-review hashes. It grants\nno post-exposure repair authority.\n\n","stream":false,"think":true}